examples.question_text:
Name the solid. Explain how you chose its name.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,line width=0.5pt,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
% Triangular prism: front vertices (0,0,0), (2.4,0,0), (1.2,1.8,0); rear vertices at z=1.
% Faces: ABC, DEF, ABED, BCFE, CADF. Projection: (x+0.9z,y+0.6z).
% Viewing direction towards viewer: (0.9,0.6,-1). Front ABC and side BCFE are visible.
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (2.4,0); \coordinate (C) at (1.2,1.8);
\coordinate (D) at (0.9,0.6); \coordinate (E) at (3.3,0.6); \coordinate (F) at (2.1,2.4);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(E)--(F)--(C);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(D)--(E);
\draw[dashed] (D)--(F);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The two identical parallel bases are triangles. Name the base first, then add prism: **triangular prism**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,line width=0.5pt,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
% Same explicit prism faces, projection and viewing direction as the question.
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (2.4,0); \coordinate (C) at (1.2,1.8);
\coordinate (D) at (0.9,0.6); \coordinate (E) at (3.3,0.6); \coordinate (F) at (2.1,2.4);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (B)--(E)--(F)--(C);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(D)--(E);
\draw[dashed] (D)--(F);
\path (1.2,-0.35) node {triangular base};
\path (3.2,2.85) node {matching base};
% Annotation guides are separate from solid boundaries.
% F is the identifiable upper vertex of rear triangular base DEF.
\draw[->,draw={rgb,255:red,38;green,140;blue,255}] (3.15,2.65)--(F);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
This solid has a rectangular base. Name it and explain your choice.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,line width=0.5pt,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
% Rectangular pyramid: A=(0,0,0), B=(3,0,0), C=(3,2,0), D=(0,2,0), P=(1.5,1,2.6).
% Faces: ABCD, ABP, BCP, CDP, DAP. Projection: (x+0.3y,z+0.2y).
% Viewing direction towards viewer: (0.3,-1,0.2). Faces ABP and BCP are visible; D is obscured.
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (3,0); \coordinate (C) at (3.6,0.4); \coordinate (D) at (0.6,0.4); \coordinate (P) at (1.8,2.8);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C);
\draw (A)--(P)--(B) (P)--(C);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(D)--(C) (D)--(P);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
There is one rectangular base. The triangular side faces meet at an apex, so the solid is a **rectangular pyramid**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,line width=0.5pt,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
% Same explicit pyramid faces, projection and viewing direction as the question.
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (3,0); \coordinate (C) at (3.6,0.4); \coordinate (D) at (0.6,0.4); \coordinate (P) at (1.8,2.8);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C);
\draw (A)--(P)--(B) (P)--(C);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(D)--(C) (D)--(P);
\path (1.8,3.1) node {apex};
\path (1.5,-0.4) node {rectangular base};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
